WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.304
Label noise: when the gold is wrong.

2
00:00:02.584 --> 00:00:06.915
Every labeled dataset contains errors, and test sets are no exception.

3
00:00:07.195 --> 00:00:11.461
Northcutt and colleagues (2021) estimated at least 3.

4
00:00:11.741 --> 00:00:21.960
3 percent label errors on average across ten widely used test sets in vision, language, and audio, and showed that correcting them could change which model looked better.

5
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:29.344
If two fine-tuned models differ by two points on a test set with three percent bad labels, you don’t yet know which is better.

6
00:00:29.624 --> 00:00:31.885
Noise does different damage in each split.

7
00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:43.664
In training, the model learns the errors: a consistent labeling mistake becomes a consistent model mistake, which is one reason guideline consistency matters more than volume.

8
00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:48.147
In test, errors cap the accuracy you can measure and blur comparisons.

9
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:52.395
Practical ways to find and reduce it: Mechanical checks first.

10
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:59.757
For extraction, the evidence-quote check from Studio 1 and the lab catches labels whose quotes aren’t in the document.

11
00:01:00.037 --> 00:01:04.944
It’s cheap and certain, and in the lab it catches a third of the planted errors on its own.

12
00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:06.824
Double labeling on a sample.

13
00:01:07.104 --> 00:01:14.869
Two people label the same examples independently; measure agreement (Studio 3, week 4) and adjudicate disagreements.

14
00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:19.416
Disagreement clusters usually point at an ambiguous guideline rule.

15
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:21.296
Model-disagreement review.

16
00:01:21.576 --> 00:01:28.339
Run a strong model over the labeled data and send the examples where it disagrees with the gold label to a person.

17
00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:40.053
This finds errors efficiently, but the person decides, not the model: otherwise you are slowly replacing your labels with the model’s opinions and then measuring the model against itself.

18
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.275
Fix the guideline, then relabel.

19
00:01:42.555 --> 00:01:49.957
When you change a rule, relabel every affected example, in all splits, and record the guideline version in the dataset card.
